Can I use C++ instead of python?

Yes. **You can use C++ instead of Python** for many of the same tasks around Mission Planner/ArduPilot.

The key point is that **Mission Planner doesn't require Python or C++**. Your external program communicates with ArduPilot using **MAVLink**.

```text
                 ArduPilot / SITL
                       │
                    MAVLink
                       │
          ┌────────────┴────────────┐
          │                         │
    Mission Planner              Your app
       (C#/.NET)               ┌────┴────┐
                               │         │
                             C++       Python
```

### What you can do with C++

| Task                          | C++ |
| ----------------------------- | --- |
| Read UAV telemetry            | ✅   |
| GPS/altitude/speed monitoring | ✅   |
| Read battery/status           | ✅   |
| Send MAVLink commands         | ✅   |
| Upload missions/waypoints     | ✅   |
| Arm/disarm                    | ✅   |
| Change flight modes           | ✅   |
| Control SITL                  | ✅   |
| Analyze flight logs           | ✅   |
| Build a custom GCS            | ✅   |
| Automate UAV testing          | ✅   |

### For your C++ learning

You could build a **C++ console UAV application** without any GUI:

```text
╔════════════════════════════════════╗
║       UAV TELEMETRY MONITOR        ║
╠════════════════════════════════════╣
║ Mode       : AUTO                  ║
║ Armed      : YES                   ║
║ Latitude   : 12.9716               ║
║ Longitude  : 77.5946               ║
║ Altitude   : 52.4 m                ║
║ Groundspeed: 14.2 m/s              ║
║ Battery    : 78 %                  ║
║ GPS        : 14 satellites         ║
╚════════════════════════════════════╝
```

Your architecture could be:

```text
C++ Console UI
       ↓
UAV Controller
       ↓
MAVLink communication
       ↓
ArduPilot SITL
       ↓
Mission Planner / QGroundControl
```

So if your goal is **C++ + UAV domain knowledge without embedded development**, this is actually a good project direction.

One caveat: C++ MAVLink integration is more hands-on than Python because Python has very convenient MAVLink tooling such as `pymavlink`. But C++ is absolutely viable.
